      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\), we start by finding the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\).\n\nFirst, we perform the long division of 1 by 7:\n1. \\( 1 \\div 7 = 0. \\) (since 7 goes into 1 zero times),\n2. \\( 10 \\div 7 = 1 \\) with a remainder of 3, so we write down 1 and bring down a 0 to make it 30.\n3. \\( 30 \\div 7 = 4 \\) with a remainder of 2, so we write down 4 and bring down a 0 to make it 20.\n4. \\( 20 \\div 7 = 2 \\) with a remainder of 6, so we write down 2 and bring down a 0 to make it 60.\n5. \\( 60 \\div 7 = 8 \\) with a remainder of 4, so we write down 8 and bring down a 0 to make it 40.\n6. \\( 40 \\div 7 = 5 \\) with a remainder of 5, so we write down 5 and bring down a 0 to make it 50.\n7. \\( 50 \\div 7 = 7 \\) with a remainder of 1, so we write down 7 and bring down a 0 to make it 10.\n8. \\( 10 \\div 7 = 1 \\) with a remainder of 3, so we write down 1 and bring down a 0 to make it 30. This process repeats.\n\nThus, the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is:\n\\[ 0.\\overline{142857} \\]\nThis means the decimal repeats every 6 digits: 142857.\n\nTo find the 100th digit after the decimal point, we need to determine the position of the 100th digit within the repeating cycle. We do this by finding the remainder when 100 is divided by 6:\n\\[ 100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\]\nThis tells us that the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit in the repeating cycle 142857. The 4th digit is 8.\n\nTherefore, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
